{"ok": false, "issues": ["The requirement and key_value misstate the added-adult-tenant rule. Under § 1946.2(a), if an adult tenant is added before an existing tenant reaches 24 months, the section applies once either all tenants have 12 months of occupancy or one or more tenants have 24 months. It is not simply a 24-month threshold. Both fields are reworded to match the statute.", "Status in_force, effective_date 2024-04-01 (operative date under subd. (m)) and end_date 2030-01-01 (repeal under subd. (n)) are correct as of 2026-10-01. The AB 1529 amendment effective January 1, 2026 does not change these dates, so there is no conflict.", "subject, yields_to_local true, preempts_local false and the citation are correct. The coverage_logic directions match the statutory exemptions; the conditions that cannot be encoded remain in the exemptions text."], "scope": "core", "applies_only_in": [], "conflict_unresolved": false, "conflict_note": null, "fix": {"requirement": "Once a tenant has continuously and lawfully occupied a unit for 12 months, the owner may terminate the tenancy only for an at-fault or no-fault just cause stated in the written termination notice. If an adult tenant was added before an existing tenant reached 24 months, this applies once all tenants have 12 months or any tenant has 24 months. For no-fault terminations, the owner must either pay relocation assistance equal to one month's rent within 15 calendar days of serving the notice or waive the final month's rent.", "key_value": "Just cause required after 12 months of occupancy (if adult tenants were added, once all tenants have 12 months or any tenant has 24 months); no-fault relocation assistance of one month's rent, paid within 15 calendar days of service of the notice, or waiver of the final month's rent"}}
